Track 2: "Did You Mend the Shroud?"
- Story & Context: In the damp basement of a defunct Morne Rouge garment factory, Vespera spent three months unpicking her grandmother’s hand-embroidered wedding dress to weave the ceremonial mourning banners for the upcoming eclipse. The air reeked of moldering cotton and wet charcoal as she tapped out the rhythmic cadence against a rusted radiator, whispering the confession directly into an antique ribbon microphone: "You wear the white for the wedding, but you mend the shroud for the dance." When her former sound system crew arrived at the factory door pleading with her to return to traditional daytime bashment, she simply turned the sub-bass amplifier to full gain and drove them onto the street with a punishing shockwave of 96-BPM sub-frequencies.
